      "name": "InitializeGameEntityResources",
      "signature": "int InitializeGameEntityResources(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "comment": "Initializes game entity resources and validates entity state.\n\nThis function performs critical setup for game entity management by validating\nall managed entities and conditionally initializing resource state based on\nthe current initialization status. It serves as a main initialization entry\npoint for the entity resource system.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Call ValidateGameEntityModes() to refresh mode state for all primary entities\n2. Call ValidateAndSetEntityMode() to validate additional entity state\n3. Check global state variable DAT_6f9e9c50 for values 0xd (13) or 0x4 (4)\n4. If already in initialized state, skip to return\n5. Set resource limit to 200 (0xc8) at global DAT_6fa08e4c\n6. Call InitializeResourceState() to allocate and initialize resource lists\n7. Set initialization flag DAT_6f9e9c40 to 1 to mark as initialized\n8. Calculate inventory offset using CalculateInventoryOffset(4) for type 4\n9. Store calculated offset in state variable DAT_6f9e9c50 as byte\n10. Clear inventory count variable DAT_6f9e9c54 to 0\n11. Return 1 to indicate successful initialization\n\nParameters:\nNone - Function operates on global entity and resource state variables\n\nReturns:\nint - Always returns 1 to indicate successful initialization\n\nState Variables Modified:\n- DAT_6f9e9c50: Current initialization state (set to inventory offset or sentinel)\n- DAT_6f9e9c40: Initialization flag (set to 1 when entering initialized state)\n- DAT_6fa08e4c: Resource limit (set to 200)\n- DAT_6f9e9c54: Inventory count (cleared to 0)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If DAT_6f9e9c50 is already 0xd (13) or 0x4 (4), initialization is skipped\n- These sentinel values indicate the system is already fully initialized\n- Resource limit of 200 is hardcoded for this initialization phase\n- Inventory offset calculation uses base offset of 4 with type mapping in CalculateInventoryOffset\n- Function uses __stdcall convention with 4-byte parameter cleanup (RET 0x4)",
